A: I don't think you want another assert statement in the ArrayListContractTest class.
Assert statement will be in a test method that later should be run separately. This will make your tests less focused and less understandable for other developers. It's better to observe changes during actual execution. So you can use PexObserve.
Also, the assertion in your code is contradictory because you compare the old position of the element to the new position of the element. If you are testing whether the element is moved, then you'd better check that index of the element is different from the old one.
Here is a corrected version of your code:
public class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    [PexCustomAttribute("IndexOf", typeof(int))]
    [PexObserve(Feature.IndexOf.Feature, "x", typeof(int))]
    [PexExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentOutOfRangeException))]
    public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
    {
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

        var Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        var Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        var Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        arrList.Set(index, x);

        var New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        var New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        var New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

        // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that index of x in new array list is different from old index of x
        Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX != New_arrListIndexOfX);
    }
}

I changed the Assert statement to observe that the IndexOf element 'x' changes from one to another: Old_arrListIndexOfX != New_arrListIndexOfX. So, if the element x is moved then IndexOf(x) should differ from the old IndexOf(x).
Also, please note that I've changed PexObserve with the right feature to observe IndexOf feature. And I've assumed you meant to observe IndexOf, not x. So the right way is to observe "IndexOf" with parameter "x" and return type int. If you meant to observe the x element then use the correct parameters "x" and typeof(int).
Hope this helps. Please let me know if you need further assistance.
