What am I missing here ? Is there any special typecasting or method which could fix this issue ?

A: You will find that the PexRunner will generate tests for the different scenarios, and one way to ensure only one instance of that number is contained in the ArrayList is to prevent the ArrayList from holding duplicate items. 

Here is an example of a class that satisfies the condition:

```
[PexClass]
[PexMethodUnderTest("System.Collections.ArrayList")]
public partial class ArrayListMembershipTest 
{
    [PexMethod]
    public void AddSingleItem_ContainsItem([PexAssumeValid]int item) 
    {
        ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
        list.Add(item);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("count", list.Count);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("contains", list.Contains(item));
        Debug.Assert(list.Count == 1 && list.Contains(item));
    }
}
```


This example tests the ArrayList's `Contains` function.  The added item should be contained within the list, and the count of the list should be 1. The Pex runner will provide tests that check against a variety of cases, not only edge cases.

Also, please note that in your example, the method under test "Put_SetContract" lacks the [PexMethod] annotation, which should be fixed.

I hope this could be helpful.

Regarding the error message, it is caused by the assumption that the index must be a valid index of the given ArrayList, which does not always hold true. The ArrayList's `IndexOf` function could return -1 if the specified value is not present in the ArrayList, which does not qualify as a valid index. 

To guard your code from failure due to invalid indexes, you can add additional checks before the operation with an invalid index is attempted. 

Keep up to learn about better coverage on your tests with the Pex runner.

Also, if you are using NUnit or MSTest as your test framework you might not be fully covered by Pex. That's where you can see that the Pex coverage is limited to .Net Core/Standard and it doesn't support the PexRunner for NUnit or MSTest tests. It's a bit tricky as it is not recommended to use Pex with NUnit or MSTest. Pex works best with the normal test method in test classes annotated with `[PexClass]` and method should be marked with `[PexMethod]`.

Please refer to the link: https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/peshk/2008/08/06/pex-runner-for-visual-studio-2010/ for more information on the use of PexRunner.

I hope this helps clarify the usage of the PE+X framework.

Happy testing!!


A: So the issue seems to be with the type casting and its corresponding IndexOf() and SetItem() method and its preconditions also. IndexOf() and SetItem() both expect valid indices. 

ArrayList indexOf returns -1 if the object is not found, and -2 if x is null. 
And here, this method can return an invalid index in some cases:

public void RemoveAt(int index)
{
  if (index < 0 || index >= count) 
    throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException("index", index);

  items[index] = null;
  count--;
  version++;
}

Therefore, you need to make sure that IndexOf() does not return an out of range index. 

For this, you may need to control the index properly, for example by using PexAssumeValid or a similar annotation.

I hope this clears it up, and please let me know if you have any other questions or concerns.


Happy coding!! 

And thanks for pointing me to the PexResources to assist me in my learning experience.


