I'm getting the error "Cannot find the contract for PutSetContract". Can anyone give a suggestion?

A: The contract method must match exactly the method that is being tested. You did not specify the methods you're testing, so can't guarantee this. 


*

*A common mistake is forgetting to include the return value of the method being tested in your contract method, so it will not match and Pex will not be able to generate a suitable contract. 

*Another common mistake is to name the contract method the same as the one being tested, but with a different return type (especially a partial method). 


Also ensure that your test projects references the correct code coverage analysis dlls, as well as any other relevant dependencies/references.
If none of the above helps, check if your tests are still running, and make sure to use PexAllowedException to catch any exceptions thrown by Pex.
If you're still in trouble, you may want to use the PexForgery attribute to generate a suitable initialisation for your arraylist.
But it is important to know what you are testing and what kind of code coverage you are aiming for, as not all tests may meet your requirements. For best results, conduct code reviews and refactor if necessary to get your tests to the coverage you want.
And remember Pex does not generate test cases, it generates input for your tests and then verifies that your test actually executes and covers the code under the input, not the other way around.
Check the generated code for your test and if the contracts are fine. 
If contract is not defined, you can define your own using PexContracts - https://github.com/Microsoft/CodeContracts/tree/master/src/Pex
This will help you to achieve a better code coverage measurement.

A: The main reason you are seeing this error message is because the contract test method PUT_SetContract does not exist in your test class. ArrayListContractTest does not have a method named PUT_SetContract.
The method PUT_SetContract corresponds to the method ArrayList.set, i.e., it is the contract for ArrayList's set method. If you want to specify a Pex contract, you would need to create a method that represents the arraylist.set method's pre-condition, transform, and post-condition.
You can find an example here:
https://github.com/Microsoft/Pex/tree/master/Samples/PexAll/Net452/PexSamples/ArrayList
Please look at the PexSampleForArrayListTest.cs file.
The other reason could be that your test is in a different project to the ArrayList project you're testing. Please make sure the test project references the ArrayList project. Also, make sure all project references to arraylist are targeting the correct version (net452 in this case) as this is important.

A: I would check several things:


*

*In your test project, check if ArrayListContractTest class is actually in the test project and not in another one.

*Do you have the latest version of Pex and are you using it against the right version of .NET framework (check the settings). 

*Check if the target framework of your test project is not set to a different version. 

*Possibly the method you are testing (ArrayList.set) is not visible to the test because it is declared outside the class under test. If the method is declared Private, it is not visible to unit testing.


In case you've checked all of the above and still getting errors, you would want to double check your PexAttribute and the pre-conditions, post-conditions etc. 
As stated in Microsoft's documentation, 
Code contracts cannot automatically find methods for Pex contracts.
If you're using other tools (like ReSharper) they can interfere with Pex. 
To ensure Pex contracts work, disable other code analysis tools on the tested project. 

Check if it works if you manually add contracts to each method, which can help you understand exactly where Pex is getting confused. 
Also make sure that your test method is set to PexInisializedState.None, as Pex might not work correctly if not set to None.
Finally, ensure the paths are correctly set in your references because it might be the case that the references are pointing to different versions of the ArrayList.
Hope this addresses the issue.

